# THE ABUSE SURFACE OF *MANAGED* IDENTITY — measured record

## HEADLINE ANSWERS

**1. Has anyone built an exit the manager cannot block? NO.** Across every shipped supervised-identity system reachable — Apple Family Sharing, Managed Apple IDs, Google Family Link, Google Workspace, Microsoft Family Safety — and every SSI guardianship design (Sovrin, Hyperledger Aries), the subject's exit is gated on (a) the manager's cooperation, (b) an age threshold *plus* a manager-controlled toggle, or (c) an external legal process. The only design where a delegated party provably cannot become the controller is **Ethereum social-recovery wallets** (Buterin, 2021-01-11, https://vitalik.eth.limo/general/2021/01/11/recovery.html) — and that is the *inverse* relation: the subject holds the signing key and can unilaterally remove guardians after a 1–3 day timelock; guardians can recover but never transact. It contains **no supervised-principal role at all**, so there is no exit to block. The correct primitive exists (recoverer ≠ controller; timelocked unilateral removal) and has never, in anything reachable, been applied to a manager→subject relation.

**2. When management is structural rather than incidental, what breaks?** The measured record says: **the identity/authorization layer becomes the abuse surface, and malware becomes a rounding error.** Havron et al. (USENIX Sec 2019, https://www.usenix.org/system/files/sec19-havron.pdf), 44 IPV clients, 105 devices, 75 ISDi scans: issues detected were **potential spyware 3 · password compromise 14 · unknown "trusted" devices 12 · shared family/phone plan 4**. Relation-layer findings outnumbered spyware **~10:1**. ISDi flagged 79 apps, 61 dual-use, and *for all but one the client recognized the app already*. The abuse ran through legitimate authority, not hidden code.

**3. Is invisible management more dangerous than visible? Asserted, not measured — and the visible/invisible axis is not the load-bearing one.** See §E. But there is a hard normative floor (UN CRC GC No. 25 ¶75) and a shipped-industry precedent (DULT) both saying an unnotified tracking relation is unacceptable.

---

## A. PARENTAL CONTROL / FAMILY TRACKING AS AN ABUSE VECTOR — the numbers

**Roundy et al., "The Many Kinds of Creepware Used for Interpersonal Attacks," IEEE S&P 2020** (PDF: https://nixdell.com/papers/Creepware_IEEE_SandP_camera.pdf; DOI 10.1109/sp40000.2020.00069)
- Dataset: **billions of app installs across 50M+ Android devices** (Norton Mobile Security), 2017 and 2018/19 windows.
- CreepRank = guilt-by-association graph mining with MAP estimation. Seed 2017: **18 surveillance apps on 35,811 devices**; seed 2018/19: **50 apps on 32,719 devices**.
- Top 1,000 hand-coded → **857 qualify as creepware**. Surveillance **372**; of those, **107 multifaceted surveillance apps affecting 172K Norton customers in 2017 alone**. Spoofing 114 · harassment 80 · hacking tutorials 63 (61 in another count) · location-only 90 · thorough 90 · social media 105 · covert social media 26 · camera 15 · mic 11 · SMS 9 · screen 5 · rooting tutorials 6.
- Disclosure: **1,095 apps reported to Google; Google determined 813 violated policy and removed them.**
- The dual-use finding, verbatim: *"many apps that purport to be intended for child online safety have highest PMI with apps that are unambiguously intended for intimate partner surveillance."* "Family Locator for Android" was **previously titled "GirlFriend Cell Tracker."** "Cell Tracker" — the most prevalent thorough-surveillance app in the top 1,000 — markets on child safety and correlates strongly with "Cheating Spouse," "Boyfriend Tracker Free," "Where the hell are you?"
- 2018/19 vs 2017: information extraction more than doubled; surveillance grew; **the fraction of surveillance apps recommended for child safety rose relative to IPS framing** — the authors read this as re-marketing in response to enforcement.

**Chatterjee et al., "The Spyware Used in Intimate Partner Violence," IEEE S&P 2018** (https://nixdell.com/papers/spyware.pdf)
- 10,000+ apps retrieved; **9,224 collected**; 1,629 active Play listings; of a hand-labeled 1,000, **280 (28%) marked IPS tools**. Off-store: **32 found, 23 active** — all overt spyware. So **~92% of the IPS-relevant corpus is on-store dual-use.**
- **"All of the off-store spyware apps we analyzed describe child safety as one of their use cases."**
- Taxonomy names the shape directly: *personal tracking / mutual tracking (family trackers) / **subordinate tracking (child monitoring apps)***. Parental control is the subordinate tier **by construction**.
- Review mining: **464,625 reviews across >9,000 apps → 103 reviews on 82 apps explicitly describing partner surveillance**; example on "SMS Tracker Plus — an app which claims to be for parental control": *"Love it!!! I've been suspecting my gf cheating and this gave me answers really quick."*
- Detection: best tool (Anti Spy Mobile) flagged **95% of off-store spyware but only 47% of on-store IPS-relevant apps**, at 12% FPR. Major AV flagged **at most 13%** of on-store dual-use.
- **One measured counter-example that works:** TeenSafe checks the age of the Google account the device is registered to; abusers complain in reviews about the difficulty. A *role/age check at the identity layer* is the only mitigation in this literature that adversaries noticed and complained about.
- Bundled/carrier tier (Verizon Family Locator, Sprint Family Locator, Find My Device, Google Maps timeline): *"remotely activated with the credentials attached to the account that pays the cellular bill"* and **"impossible to uninstall as they are bundled with the OS; at best they can be disabled."**

**Maier, Tanczer & Klausner, "Surveillance Disguised as Protection," PoPETs 2025(2):100–120**, DOI 10.56553/popets-2025-0052 (PDF: https://petsymposium.org/popets/2025/popets-2025-0052.pdf; arXiv 2504.16087)
- **20 sideloaded vs 20 Play Store** parental-control apps, Android 9.0, static + dynamic + traffic + policy analysis.
- Permissions: sideloaded mean **44.4** (range 22–70, mSpy highest) vs in-store **34.9** (15–58). Dangerous permissions: **21.0 vs 11.8**.
- **Every** sideloaded app abused the Accessibility API (14/20 in-store did). **Device Administrator: 16 sideloaded, 15 in-store** — used *primarily to block uninstallation*. **Draw-Over-Other-Apps: 13 sideloaded, 14 in-store** — used to close the settings app when uninstall is attempted. Kidlogger minimizes the settings window on every attempt to reach its uninstall button.
- **17 of 20 sideloaded apps instruct the user to disable Google Play Protect**; 13 were nonetheless detected by Play Protect 42.1.27-31; **7 were not: Bark, EvaSpy, FlexiSpy, Spapp Monitoring, SPYX, TheOneSpy, TiSpy**.
- **17 of 20 obfuscated their name** ("Settings", "System Service", "WiFi Service", "Sync Services"); 17 used an innocuous icon. Zero in-store apps obfuscated (Play policy forbids it).
- **Stalkerware IOC match: 8/20 (paper body) — note the PoPETs landing page abstract says 9/20; the PDF says 8. Discrepancy flagged.** Against TinyCheck: **25% of all sideloaded parental control apps identified, 40% of the top 20**. Against Echap's IOC DB: **50% of the 44 identified sideloaded parental control apps.**
- 3 apps sent sensitive data unencrypted; 10 of 20 sideloaded lacked an applicable privacy policy.
- **12 of 20 sideloaded apps promote monitoring of Tinder/Hinge** — platforms with an 18+ floor. The authors: this "suggests these services could be used to track adults' activities rather than children's… while also risking outing minors who are, for example, 'closeted' LGBTQ+ users."
- **Consent finding, structural:** citing Parsons et al. (Citizen Lab 2019), *"obtaining consent from just one parent is inadequate in joint parenting situations… **None of the analysed apps implemented measures to ensure this.**"* And: *"Some software vendors stated that only the device owner's consent is required for monitoring. However, device ownership extends beyond legal guardianship."*

**ISDi's live blocklist — a direct measurement of the shape of the surface** (https://github.com/stopipv/isdi, `src/isdi/data/app-flags.csv`, HEAD 2026-07-22)
- **5,777 flagged apps. dual-use 5,150 (89.1%) · stalkerware 613 · spyware 13 · safe 1.**
- By store: playstore 5,084 · appstore 648 · offstore 45. **Only 45 of 5,777 are off-store.**
- **581 (10.1%) carry `family|parent|kid|child|teen` in the title** — 569 of those flagged dual-use.
- The clinic's operational model of the abuse surface is 89% *legitimate-purpose software*, 99.2% on-store.

**Tseng et al., "The Tools and Tactics Used in Intimate Partner Surveillance," USENIX Sec 2020** (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.14341)
- 5 forums, **18,937 threads / ~200K posts**, first thread Jan 2006, crawl through 2018. Qualitative coding of **750 threads (250 each from 3 non-spam forums)**.
- IPS-relevant share of threads: Forum A 78% · B 51% · C 18%. **Threads that escalate: 32% / 38% / 35%.**
- Two of five forums are essentially spyware SEO spam: Forum D's top user touched **95% of threads and authored 45% of posts**; 94% of that user's posts were spam.
- **Family plans and Apple IDs are named attack tools by attackers themselves.** Verbatim from Forum A: *"We are in the process of choosing new cellphones and a new company. Which is the best company to keep tabs, records, etc? We currently have iPhones on AT&T, and their **Family Map** did help me prove his affair."* Responder recommends staying with AT&T for "immediate online access [to phone records] and their GPS is good."
- Also: iCloud-without-device-access (*"What is the best spyware if I can't get their phone, but have their Apple ID and password?"*), a family iPad synced to a partner's iCloud, Significant Locations, Find My Friends, Google Maps Timeline, and *"mobile spyware products that leveraged cloud-based access… **such as tools marketed for use in parental control contexts**."*

**Teen-side: monitoring does not reduce risk, and teens rate it 1.79 stars**
- **Ghosh, Badillo-Urquiola, Guha, LaViola, Wisniewski, "Safety vs. Surveillance," CHI 2018**, DOI 10.1145/3173574.3173698 (PDF: https://www.cs.ucf.edu/icerc/isuelab/publications/pubs/pn1838-ghoshA.pdf). Scraped Aug 2016 from **66 apps, 29,272 reviews** → **736 child-authored reviews across 37 apps**, reviewer ages 8–19. **Child mean 1.79 (SD 1.48); presumed-parent mean 3.66 (SD 1.79) over 28,536 reviews; difference 1.866, 95% CI [1.76,1.98], t(793.34)=33.77, p<.05. 76% of child reviews are 1★.** Coded complaints in the 79% "disliked" set: **Overly Restrictive 35% · Privacy Invasive 23% (codes include *stalking*) · Bad Parenting/Lack of Communication 14% · Faulty Design 14%.** **The word "abuse" appears zero times in the paper**; the strongest published framing is "parental stalking," "asymmetric power differentials," and violation of contextual integrity.
- **Ghosh et al., "A Matter of Control or Safety?", CHI 2018**, DOI 10.1145/3173574.3173768. **215 matched parent–teen dyads (13–17)**, two logistic regressions. **Low-autonomy-granting (authoritarian) parents most likely to use the apps**; teens victimized online and with peer problems more likely to be monitored; **"increased parental control was associated with MORE (not fewer) online risks."** (Cross-sectional; effect sizes NOT REACHED — ACM 403.)
- **Wang, Zhao, Van Kleek, Shadbolt, "Protection or Punishment?", CSCW 2021**, DOI 10.1145/3476084, arXiv 2109.05347. Independent replication: **58 apps, 93,404 reviews → 81,488 deduped → 3,264 retained: 746 child, 2,518 parent.** Same qualitative verdict.
- **Stoilova, Bulger & Livingstone (2023)**, DOI 10.1080/17482798.2023.2265512: rapid evidence review, **1,656 articles screened, 40 coded**. *"the consequences of use were mixed… evidence of parental controls having both beneficial and adverse outcomes, limiting other outcomes or simply having no outcomes"*; **"little support for advocating parental controls as a stand-alone strategy."**
- **Ghosh, Hughes & Wisniewski, "Circle of Trust," CHI 2020**, DOI 10.1145/3313831.3376747: within-subjects study, **17 parent–child pairs**. Both parents *and* children significantly preferred a less-invasive design over the traditional parental-control baseline. Families valuing **privacy, trust, freedom, balance of power** preferred it; families valuing **transparency and control** preferred the status quo.

**Prevalence of family location tracking**
- Pew 2016 (https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2016/01/07/parents-teens-and-digital-monitoring/): **16% of parents of 13–17s use monitoring tools to track the teen's location**; 61% check browsing history; 48% look through call/text records.
- Pew 2020 (N=3,640 US parents, fielded 2020-03-02→15): for a child 5–11, **33% track location via GPS apps/software**; 75% check sites/apps; 49% read call records or texts.
- Davis et al. 2024, DOI 10.1007/s10567-024-00500-8: **"33–69% of U.S. families"** use digital location tracking; the field is **"vastly understudied."**
- Boele, Bülow & Keijsers (accepted, J. Adolescence; PsyArXiv 10.31234/osf.io/mzry3_v2): **525 Dutch dyads (2024) + 175 replication (2019)**. **99.6% of parents tracked ≥1 domain**; school performance 96.4%, finances 90.1%, **location 65.7%**. Adolescent acceptance: **location tracking rejected by ~a quarter**, significantly less accepted than school/finance (χ²(2)=220.8, p<.001). **Tracking was UNRELATED to adolescent wellbeing but POSITIVELY associated with parental overprotection, control, and "dangerous worldviews."** **In 4–7% of dyads the adolescent believed they were NOT tracked while the parent reported tracking** — measured invisible management inside consumer systems.
- Chase et al. 2024, DOI 10.1002/jad.12300 (N=706 college students): **"nearly half… currently or previously being digitally location tracked by their parent/caregiver."** Currently-tracked students perceived **more helicopter parenting and less autonomy support**.
- Burnell et al. 2023, DOI 10.1037/fam0001067 (N=729 adolescents, M=15.03): ~half tracked; associations with externalizing/alcohol **were not robust** to multi-informant checks or statistical correction.

**Life360** — Keegan & Ng, The Markup, **2021-12-06**, https://themarkup.org/privacy/2021/12/06/the-popular-family-safety-app-life360-is-selling-precise-location-data-on-its-tens-of-millions-of-user (note: slug ends `-user`, singular). **33M users**; ~a dozen data partners (X-Mode/Outlogic, Cuebiq, SafeGraph, Veraset, Arity, Jiobit); data-sale revenue **$693K (2016) → $16M (2020), ~20% of annual revenue**, plus $6M from Arity. Sold raw **lat/long + MAID + IP**. CEO Chris Hulls: *"data is an important part of our business model that allows us to keep the core Life360 services free."* A former X-Mode engineer called Life360 data among X-Mode's most valuable offerings for volume and precision. Follow-ups: **2022-01-27** partial retreat (https://themarkup.org/privacy/2022/01/27/life360-says-it-will-stop-selling-precise-location-data) — stops broker sales, **keeps precise sharing with Arity and aggregated with Placer.ai**, no completion date; **2023-06-01** class action (filed 2023-01-12, N.D. Cal., lead plaintiff **a Florida minor and his family**; complaint names inference of *"places that may be used to infer an LGBTQ+ identification,"* domestic abuse shelters, medical facilities). Downstream: **FTC v. X-Mode/Outlogic, 2024-01-09, 3–0 vote**, order requires deletion of previously collected location data and bars associating data with LGBTQ+ locations or protests. **No FTC or state-AG action naming Life360 itself — NOT REACHED / apparently none.**

**Citizen Lab, "The Predator in Your Pocket," 2019-06-12**, 165pp, https://citizenlab.ca/2019/06/the-predator-in-your-pocket-a-multidisciplinary-assessment-of-the-stalkerware-application-industry/ — 8 apps assessed (FlexiSPY, Highster Mobile, Hoverwatch, Mobistealth, mSpy, TeenSafe, TheTruthSpy, Cerberus). Reports the widely-recirculated NNEDV shelter-worker figures (85% GPS, 71% computer monitoring, 54% stalkerware) — **provenance caution: these are practitioner surveys of shelter staff, not victim-level prevalence.**

---

## B. THE EXIT — the record is uniformly negative

| System | Manager's powers | Subject exit | Manager can block? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Apple Family Sharing, adult | organizer can remove | **yes, unilateral** | no |
| **Apple Family Sharing, 13–17 with Screen Time on** | Screen Time, location, purchase approval, activity | only after the organizer disables Screen Time | **YES, documented** |
| **Apple Family Sharing, child <13** | full | none — org must delete or transfer the account | **YES, total** |
| Apple Safety Check | n/a | out of scope | Screen Time/MDM degrade it |
| **Managed Apple ID (ABM/ASM)** | reset password, deactivate, delete, transfer, force sign-out, read logs | **none documented** | **YES, total** |
| **Google Family Link 13–17** | full | **"Children need parent approval to stop supervision until they turn 18"** | **YES — it is a heading** |
| **Google family group, supervised member** | — | **"you can't leave your family group"** | **YES** |
| **Google Workspace user** | reset password + cookies, suspend, delete, take file ownership *without involving the owner*, Vault search/export/hold, full calendar access | **none** | **YES, total** |
| Sovrin SSI guardianship | guardian is credential *holder*; dependent holds no key | right asserted at governance Layer Four; **no mechanism** | **YES in practice** |
| Aries RFC 0103 | same; hazard named | revocation is issuer-side; details are a TODO | **YES** |
| W3C DID Core | no supervised-principal model at all | n/a | n/a |
| Social-recovery wallet | guardians recover, cannot transact | **subject removes guardians unilaterally, 1–3 day delay** | **NO — but no supervised role exists** |

Sources: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102652 · https://support.apple.com/guide/personal-safety/safety-check-iphone-ios-16-ips2aad835e1/web (published Mar 2026) · https://support.apple.com/guide/personal-safety/safety-check-faq-ips5f85e089f/web (Oct 2024) · https://support.apple.com/guide/apple-business-manager/about-managed-apple-accounts-axm78b477c81/web (Mar 31 2025) · https://support.google.com/families/answer/7106787 · https://support.google.com/families/answer/6317858 · https://support.google.com/a/answer/2405986 · https://support.google.com/a/answer/1247799 · https://sovrin.org/wp-content/uploads/Guardianship-Whitepaper-V2.0.pdf (v2, Apr/May 2023) · https://github.com/hyperledger/aries-rfcs/tree/main/concepts/0103-indirect-identity-control

**Apple Safety Check's real scope** (the closest shipped "exit"): Emergency Reset stops person-to-person sharing across **11 Apple apps**, revokes **15 third-party permission classes**, removes signed-in devices, resets the Apple Account password and trusted numbers. Apple's own "Beyond Safety Check" list excludes non-Apple accounts, social media, and devices signed into a different Apple Account. And: *"If your iPhone has Screen Time restrictions turned on or has a mobile device management (MDM) profile installed, you can still use Safety Check but some options may not be available."* **Family Sharing, Screen Time, Managed Apple IDs, child accounts and organizer authority appear nowhere in the Safety Check flow.** It is a *peer*-relationship tool with zero authority over the *supervisory* relation.

**⚠ Two documentation contradictions found — both are instrument-lies in the safety-facing direction.**
1. Apple's Personal Safety guide (the page an abuse victim is sent to) says *"Any family member over the age of 13 can remove themselves from a family group at any time."* The operational page 102652 says a 13–17-year-old with Screen Time on **cannot leave until the organizer turns Screen Time off**. The safety-facing doc over-promises.
2. Google's https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/1350409 says *"When a child reaches their country's minimum age, they can manage their own account."* https://support.google.com/families/answer/7106787 says **"Children need parent approval to stop supervision until they turn 18."** Both live.

Apple has documented "the supervised subject cannot exit" **continuously from December 2020 to March 2026** — the December 2020 *Device and Data Access when Personal Safety is At Risk* manual (pdfinfo CreationDate Thu Dec 17 13:37:05 2020 PST, 20pp) carries the identical child-account sentence, and states it as a **security feature**, not a gap.

**Sovrin is honest about it.** Its requirement is *"Support revocation so that each person can reclaim their self-sovereign identity as and when they are able,"* and the Sovrin Governance Framework says guardians *"must respect the fundamental right of a dependent to reclaim self-sovereign identity."* But architecturally: *"The Guardian does not use the Dependent's DID as part of any of this interaction."* The dependent is the credential **subject**, never the **holder**; it holds no key. Revocation is issuer-side under a governance framework. The paper explicitly parks the exit at **Layer Four (Governance Frameworks)**: *"Above all, they should ensure a dependent's right to independence, revoking any guardianship arrangement, if necessary."* The exit is delegated to law.

**Aries RFC 0103 names the hazard and then does not solve it:** *"Since guardianship does not always derive from dependent consent… the dependent in a guardianship relationship is **particularly vulnerable to abuse from within**."* Its conclusion is *audit trails and legal formalities*. The `guardianship-details.md` file is a **73-word stub** with an empty "Privacy Considerations" heading and an open `[TODO: discuss offline mode, freshness, and revocation]`. **The guardianship-termination design in Aries is unwritten.**

**And the practitioner exit is abandonment, gated on money.** Freed et al. CHI 2018 (89 participants: 39 survivors in 11 focus groups + 50 professionals; https://nixdell.com/papers/stalkers-paradise-intimate.pdf): the first advice professionals give is *buy a new device you own* — *"in many cases, the abuser is the sole financial provider for the family and the survivor is unable to afford the cost of a new device or mobile plan."* Case worker: *"If I leave you, you're going to cut my phone off… So now, you're controlling my phone. If I don't do as you say, you're going to cut my phone off. I've seen that happen."*

**Exit is also itself dangerous.** Freed et al. CSCW 2019 (31 clients, https://nixdell.com/papers/Freed-CSCW-2019.pdf): consultants deliberately *delay* severance — *"an abuser who is used to having access to the victim's information might get angry and escalate the abuse (e.g., resort to physical violence) if that access is suddenly cut off."* Daffalla et al. 2026 (31 participants, 59 investigations): clinician-participants (C4–C7) advised **not** removing an adversarial passkey without safety planning, because *"removing access may notify the adversary, possibly escalating abuse."* Levy & Schneier (J. Cybersecurity 6(1), tyaa006, 2020, https://academic.oup.com/cybersecurity/article/6/1/tyaa006/5849222) call this the **"blank slate problem"** and criticize Apple for notifying a partner when location sharing stops; they advocate **"discreet changes to privacy preferences."**

**Bellini, "Paying the Price," CHI 2023**, DOI 10.1145/3544548.3581101 — **158 survivors (174 consultations) + 16 financial advocates**, ~45,000 words of clinic records, searches Oct 2021 / Feb 2022 / Aug 2022. Five attack categories, first of which is **ownership-/authorized-user-based**. The exact shape: S51 was an authorized user on her ex-husband's credit account; *"her ex-husband continuously threatened her with removal from his account should she do something that upset him."* And S21: *"everything is 100% under his name… **I was invisible to the banks and utility providers**."* Design finding: *"Despite often being designed into the fabric of many financial products (e.g., joint, family accounts), users who have a close intimate relationship with another user are **rarely considered as adversarial**."* **Conservatorship/power-of-attorney: zero substantive hits in the paper.** Bellini calls for adversarial thinking; **she does not propose an unblockable exit.**

---

## C. THE IDENTITY SYSTEM ITSELF AS THE ABUSE CAPABILITY — documented cases

**The canonical statement**, Freed et al. CHI 2018: *"prior work on account sharing assumes that the primary user and the owner (or controller) of the account are one and the same, **which is often not the case in IPV**. As a result, existing countermeasures may not be effective."* Their taxonomy's first category is **"Ownership-based access."** Measured: **n=27 reported abusers controlling their accounts, frequently via a shared family plan**; **n=47 accounts of location tracking via Find My Phone / parental tracking / Find My Friends**; **≥10 cases of abusers giving devices to shared children** — *"since the abuser is legally entitled to contact the child, the victim may not be allowed to remove the device."*

**Freed et al. CSCW 2019, 31 clients:** **4 clients still on a shared family plan with the abuser or the abuser's relative** *after physically leaving*; one couldn't remember her cellular-account credentials — *"Many years we've had the same account."* **12 of 31 reported abuse routed through proxies including children.** *"In many cases, children's devices were owned and had been set up by the abuser"* — and P18: *"The courts ruled that [my child] is entitled to this phone [from the abuser]. I tried to get rid of this phone completely. I did not want any part of it."* In one case *"the abuser had full access to the client's iCloud through her child's iPad"* and the client declined a new iCloud rather than lose hundreds of dollars of purchases. Also: recovery-as-lockout — *"When I try to do a password reset, a lot of them, they want access to a number that I no longer have."*

**School-issued accounts — the cleanest documented case of the identity system itself doing the harm.** CDT, *Hidden Harms*, August 2022 (PDF via Wayback: https://cdt.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Hidden-Harms-The-Misleading-Promise-of-Monitoring-Students-Online-Research-Report-Final-Accessible.pdf). Nationally representative, fielded **May/June 2022**: **1,606 parents, 1,008 teachers, students n=400 and n=460.**
- **13% of all students at schools doing activity monitoring report that they or an LGBTQ+ student they know has been outed by it — 29% among LGBTQ+ students.**
- 51% of parents and 57% of teachers agree monitoring could out LGBTQ+ students.
- 95% of teachers report their district provided/maintained devices; ~90% report the school uses monitoring software.
- LGBTQ+ students far more likely to report someone got in trouble via monitoring (**56% vs 40%**).
- **One in five parents do not know if their school uses it.**
- Chilling effect by subgroup: students with physical disabilities (n=48) **67% vs 46%** agree "I do not share my true thoughts or ideas because I know what I do online may be monitored."
- Scale of the vendor layer: EFF, 2023-10-31, https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2023/10/how-goguardian-invades-student-privacy — **GoGuardian monitors ~27 million students across ~10,000 schools; students cannot opt out.**
- Named remediation: The 74, 2023-01-28, https://www.the74million.org/article/gaggle-drops-lgbtq-keywords-from-student-surveillance-tool-following-bias-concerns/ — Gaggle removed LGBTQ keywords from its alert dictionary in late 2022.
- **The mechanism is not a rogue admin. It is the legitimate exercise of admin authority over an account the student cannot leave.**

**The identity layer is silently non-enumerable — measured across 19 services.** Daffalla, Bhattacharya, Chatterjee, Dell, *"A Framework for Abusability Analysis: The Case of Passkeys in Interpersonal Threat Models,"* USENIX Sec 2025 (https://nixdell.com/papers/2025-USENIX-abusability.pdf). 19 services, 4 passkey providers, 4 client platforms, 5 stepthrough protocols, 7 abuse vectors.
- **Adversarial biometrics: 30 stepthroughs (10 services × 3 platforms), succeeded for all.** *"none of the OSes inform users of any changes to their biometric configurations through notifications."* Windows 10's UI shows **nothing** about the number or nature of registered fingerprints.
- **Passkey cloning: for CVS, Porkbun and TikTok the access is undetectable and "we could determine no way to revoke access."**
- **Only eBay revokes passkeys on password change** — and that is, unintuitively, the only way to revoke a cloned one. No service prompts about passkeys during password reset. **No service lists which passkey authenticated a session.** For Adobe, GitHub, LinkedIn and Samsung, sessions survived revocation past 60 minutes.
- **The manager-persistence bug, exactly:** in Apple's shared-passwords group, *"the account that adds a credential to the group is the only one authorized to remove it."* If an abuser compels the victim to set up a passkey on a device signed into the **abuser's** Apple ID and then shares it to the victim's, *"there would be no way for the victim to delete the attacker's copy."*
- Follow-on: Daffalla, Myers, Bellini, *"Maybe there's only one passkey?"* USENIX Sec 2026 (https://nixdell.com/papers/2026_USENIX_adversarial_passkeys.pdf), **31 participants, 59 investigations** on Google/PayPal/LinkedIn: *"most participants were not able to identify the adversarial passkey without assistance."*

---

## D. AGES AND CONSENT

**The binding normative statement exists, and it is not a product.** UN Committee on the Rights of the Child, **General Comment No. 25 (2021)**, CRC/C/GC/25, dated **2 March 2021** (https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3906061/files/CRC_C_GC_25-EN.pdf):

- **¶75:** *"Any digital surveillance of children… should not be conducted routinely, indiscriminately or **without the child's knowledge** or, in the case of very young children, that of their parent or caregiver; nor should it take place **without the right to object to such surveillance**, in commercial settings and educational and care settings."*
- **¶76:** *"Technologies that monitor online activities for safety purposes, such as tracking devices and services, if not implemented carefully, **may prevent a child from accessing a helpline or searching for sensitive information**."*
- **¶77:** *"Protecting a child's privacy in the digital environment may be vital in circumstances where **parents or caregivers themselves pose a threat to the child's safety** or where they are **in conflict over the child's care**."*
- **¶78:** *"Providers of preventive or counselling services to children in the digital environment should be **exempt from any requirement for a child user to obtain parental consent** in order to access such services."*

That is the closest thing anywhere to a requirement for a channel the manager can neither see nor block — and it is a **state obligation, discharged by no shipped system**.

**The rights-framework literature does not fill the gap.** *Child Rights by Design* (Livingstone & Pothong, 5Rights/Digital Futures Commission, 2023, https://researchonline.lse.ac.uk/id/eprint/119724/1/Livingstone_child_rights_by_design_published.pdf), Principle 11 (Agency): agency means *"being able to start and stop using digital products and services of their choice easily"* — but framed against **commercial** exploitation, not parental authority. And Gnanasekaran & De Moor's parental-control best practices (EICC 2023, DOI 10.1145/3590777.3590800) recommend *"granting **parents** access to the children's data to delete or restrict data collection"* — the delete right is assigned to the manager, again.

**The at-risk-user taxonomy files children on the wrong side of its own distinction.** Warford et al., *"SoK: A Framework for Unifying At-Risk User Research,"* IEEE S&P 2022, meta-analysis of **95 papers** (arXiv 2112.07047). It has the exact vocabulary — *"Relationship with the attacker… attackers are likely to have physical access to the target's devices and accounts, **implicit or explicit authority over the target**"* — and files that under intimate-partner abuse. Children and teens go under a **separate, deliberately benign-intent** factor: *"**Reliance on a third party**… **Unlike** intimate threats associated with a relationship with the attacker, the third parties in this context typically have **helpful or safety-focused reasons** for 'privacy invasions'."* The SoK **cites both Ghosh CHI 2018 papers** and still codes parental monitoring as helpful-intent third-party reliance. **The literature has not named the manager-as-adversary case for children.**

**No measured causal record of a child harmed by inability to exit.** Precondition is measured (Trevor Project 2023, N=28,524, fielded 2022-09-01→12-12: only **38% found home LGBTQ-affirming**; suicide attempts **21%** where nobody in the household respects pronouns vs **12%** where all do; **27%** of those who wanted mental-health care but couldn't access it cited fear of being outed. GLSEN *Out Online* 2013, final N=5,680, fielded 2010–Jan 2011: **LGBT youth 3× as likely to not feel safe AT HOME, 16% vs 6%**; sexual harassment more common at home than school, **36% vs 26%**; **29% more out online than in person**). The *fear* is measured (Geeng & Hiniker 2021, 11 interviews; Geeng et al. USENIX Sec 2022, 14 interviews — accidental outing named as the distinctive queer threat model). **The causal link — "monitoring tool X outed N youth, outcomes Y" — is measured in exactly one place: the CDT 13%/29% figures above.** Nothing measures children who could not exit a parent-created account and what happened.

---

## E. THE CORRECTION — invisible management, blind veils, non-enumerability

**A. The three big shipped family systems all chose visible management — but the documentation is written entirely from the supervisor's seat, and the one enforceable disclosure rule carves the parent out.**

- **FTC v. Retina-X, 2019-10-22**, Decision and Order https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/cases/1723118retinaxorder_0.pdf. **Section I.C, Icon Notice** requires a visible icon plus name, clickable to a clear-and-conspicuous notice of the product's name, functions, that it is running, and how to contact the respondents. Then **I.C.b, the exception**: *"Respondents may program the Monitoring Product or Service to allow the purchaser… to **disable the Icon Notice** only if the purchaser attests, prior to installation, that the purchaser is the legal guardian or parent of a minor Child."* The gate is an **unverified self-attestation checkbox**. Permitted purposes are three: parent→minor child; employer→employee **who has given express written consent**; adult→adult **who has given express written consent**. **A child is the only supervised class whose awareness is neither consented to nor guaranteed.** Every other notice duty in the order runs to the *purchaser* (Home Page Notice, Purchase Page Notice), not the monitored person. Scale: **>15,000 subscriptions** across MobileSpy, PhoneSheriff and TeenShield before sales ceased in 2018. FTC v. SpyFone, **2021-09-01**: full ban plus a requirement to **notify owners of devices on which the apps were installed** — a remedy imposed *after the fact*, on a vendor, by an enforcer.
- **Apple Screen Time**: the child is shown that *a limit exists* (they hit it and can file an exception request the parent answers). **No Apple page reached states the child is told who set it, or notified when settings change.** The Screen Time passcode is an enforcement asymmetry, not a disclosure.
- **Microsoft Family Safety**: the hub page indexes activity reporting as *"See your child's device and app use"* — parent-directed. **Whether the child sees it: NOT REACHED** (every article-body URL 404s). The hub carries **no article about notifying the child.**
- **Google Family Link**: https://families.google/familylink/ contains **no transparency statement toward the child**; the only notification language is parent-directed (*"when your child arrives or leaves a certain location"*). All `support.google.com/families` answer pages and the Play Developer Policy stalkerware section returned CAPTCHA to both agents that tried. **The persistent-notification / unique-icon requirement and its "designed and marketed for parents to track their children" exemption is NOT REACHED — not filled from memory.**
- **Measured invisibility inside consumer systems:** Boele et al., 525 Dutch dyads, **in 4–7% of dyads adolescents believed they were NOT tracked while the parent reported tracking** (and 3–12% the reverse).

**Verdict on the sharpest contrast as posed:** the claim that "the three biggest shipped family systems all chose visible management" is **not supported by the documentation reachable here**. What is supported: they chose *visible restriction* (the subject collides with limits) and they document nothing about *visible management* (who manages, when settings change). The only rule that ever required the subject be told is the FTC's Icon Notice, whose sole exception is precisely the parent/child case, disabled on an unverified checkbox.

**B. Opacity as an abuse enabler — the field's clearest commitment is DULT, and it is a *spec*, not a measurement.**
- **IETF DULT WG** (Security Area), charter-ietf-dult-01 approved, https://datatracker.ietf.org/wg/dult/about/. Rationale: preventing misuse of tracking accessories for unauthorized surveillance, safeguards needed when a device is *"separated from a paired/owner device."* Milestones dated **July 2025**.
- `draft-ietf-dult-accessory-protocol-00`, authors **Brent Ledvina (Apple), David Lazarov (Google), Ben Detwiler (Apple), Siddika Parlak Polatkan (Google)**; latest revision **2024-11-03**; datatracker last updated **2025-05-07**; **status: EXPIRED**. Stated aim: protect *"privacy and safety of individuals in the circumstance that those accessories are used to track their location **without their knowledge or consent**."*
- `draft-ietf-dult-finding-01` ("Finding Tracking Tags", Fossaceca & Rescorla), latest revision **2025-06-06**, **EXPIRED, intended RFC status "(None)"**.
- So: **Apple and Google jointly declared that an invisible tracking relation is unacceptable and must be made visible — and as of the reachable record both drafts have expired without reaching RFC.** That is the field's clearest *statement*, and it is a statement, not a result.
- **Covert vs overt in the measured literature:** covert is *treated* as the more harmful class (Chatterjee's whole taxonomy turns on it; Maier's 17/20 obfuscation finding; Roundy's 26 explicitly-covert social-media surveillance apps). But no reachable study **compares outcomes** between covertly-monitored and overtly-monitored subjects. The proposition "invisible management is more dangerous than visible management" is **ASSERTED by industry, policy and paper framing; not measured.**
- **Counter-pressure, measured:** Freed CSCW 2019 and Daffalla 2026 both find that *making severance visible* triggers escalation risk, and clinicians therefore delay it. Levy & Schneier explicitly recommend **discreet** privacy changes and criticize Apple's stop-sharing notification. **Visibility of the management relation and visibility of its severance are opposite-signed.** Any design that couples them inherits the worse half of both.

**C. Non-enumerability — measured, and it is a *safety* cost, not only an audit cost.**
- **CETA's ISDi exists because enumeration is hard**, and it works badly: 79 flagged apps across 75 scans, **61 dual-use, 18 false positives, only 1 true covert positive; actual spyware found for 3 of 44 clients**. The findings that mattered came from the **technograph** — a hand-drawn map of people, devices and accounts — not the scanner: **4 clients with unknown "trusted" devices on iCloud, 8 more with unknown or abuser device logins on email/social, 4 on shared family plans, 14 with abuser-known passwords.** *"In some cases, the TAQ and technograph were the primary (or only) way to uncover a potential problem."* An advocate enumerating a client's exposure today does it **by interview**, because the systems do not expose the relation set.
- **Machine-level non-enumerability is measured:** no service lists which passkey authenticated a session (19 services); Windows shows nothing about registered fingerprints; no OS notifies on biometric configuration change; Apple's iCloud device list *"no additional indicators describe which devices have a copy of the passkeys and which do not."*
- **Coercive control law does turn on evidence.** Serious Crime Act 2015 s.76 (https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2015/9/section/76), in force **2015-12-29**, amended **2023-04-05**: requires repeated or continuous controlling/coercive behaviour, a "personally connected" relationship — **which expressly includes people who share parental responsibility for a child, and relatives** — a serious effect, and knowledge or constructive knowledge. Max **5 years on indictment**. The offence is proved by a *pattern*, and the pattern is normally evidenced by artefacts. **Prosecution/conviction statistics: NOT REACHED.** No source reached comments directly on what happens evidentially when the tooling leaves no discoverable trace — but the statutory structure makes the inference unavoidable: a management relation that leaves no record leaves nothing to prove a pattern with. Note also that **s.76's "personally connected" definition already reaches the parent-of-a-shared-child and the relative** — the exact relations this design makes structural.

---

## THINGS THAT CHANGE THE SHAPE OF THE DESIGN, NOT THE ANSWER INSIDE IT

1. **The measured surface is the authorization graph, not the software.** 89.1% of the clinic's blocklist is dual-use; 99.2% is on-store; 30 of ~33 detected client problems were credential/trusted-device/family-plan, versus 3 spyware. A design that makes management structural is not adding an app to the threat model — it *is* the threat model.
2. **The one mitigation adversaries visibly complained about was an identity-layer role check** (TeenSafe's account-age verification), not any UI notice.
3. **Nobody verifies who the manager is.** None of 40 parental-control apps implemented multi-guardian consent; vendors assert *"only the device owner's consent is required"*; the FTC's own remedy accepts an unverified checkbox. A design in which the manager is a **persona with no human owner-of-record** removes the last thing every existing system nominally relies on — an attributable person to attest.
4. **Non-enumerability is a documented harm channel with no counterexample.** Every measured case where a relation could not be enumerated (cloned passkeys on CVS/Porkbun/TikTok; adversarial biometrics; Apple shared-password groups; unknown iCloud trusted devices) produced *unrecoverable* adversarial persistence. Nothing in the record shows non-enumerability protecting anyone.
5. **But visibility is not free.** Severance that notifies the manager is measured as an escalation trigger, and clinicians therefore withhold it. The design decision is not "visible vs invisible" — it is **which direction each of the two visibilities points**: the management relation legible to the *managed*, the severance illegible to the *manager*. No shipped system does both. No paper reached proposes it.
6. **The five-year hole is the shape to look at.** Google states it as a heading: **"Children need parent approval to stop supervision until they turn 18,"** while the age of consent is 13–16. Apple's is 13–17 with Screen Time. **The consent age and the exit age are different numbers in every system reached**, and the gap is manager-controlled.
7. **UN CRC GC25 ¶75/¶77/¶78 is the only text anywhere that requires (a) the child know they are surveilled, (b) a right to object, and (c) a counselling channel exempt from parental consent — explicitly because "parents or caregivers themselves pose a threat."** It has zero implementations.

## NOT REACHED
- Matthews et al. CHI 2017 full text (ACM 403; **N=15 survivors**, confirmed via Freed et al. 2018's citation; three phases confirmed from the abstract).
- Freed et al. CHI 2018 was NOT REACHED by one subagent (it *was* reached and read directly by me at https://nixdell.com/papers/stalkers-paradise-intimate.pdf — all its numbers above are first-hand).
- Google Play Developer Policy stalkerware text and its parental-tracking exemption (CAPTCHA, both attempts).
- Google Family Link child-facing disclosure pages (CAPTCHA).
- Microsoft Family Safety activity-report article body (404s).
- Google Security Checkup scope; Apple "What Managed Apple Accounts can't access."
- Ghosh et al. CHI 2018 "Control or Safety?" effect sizes (ACM 403) — the "more control → more risk" finding is verified from the publisher abstract only.
- s.76 prosecution/conviction statistics; Home Office 2023 statutory guidance.
- Measured evaluations of AirTag/DULT alert efficacy; AirTag stalking incident counts.
- Slupska & Tanczer full text; Blackwell et al. 2016; Scheuerman; Hardy & Vargas.
- Any measured study of Life360 / Find My / Family Link used *coercively*; any FTC or state-AG action naming Life360.
- Any documented incident of a Google Workspace **admin** (as opposed to monitoring software running on the managed account) outing a student.
