# ADDENDUM 2 — §E corrected. The disclosure thread completed after stalling.

## ⚠️ First: an instrument-lie, caught and confirmed

Asked about Apple's App Store Review Guidelines, the WebFetch summarizer returned this as if quoted policy:

> *"Apps cannot monitor or track other users without explicit consent. Parental control apps and similar monitoring tools must: Obtain clear, informed consent from users being monitored… Not operate covertly or without the knowledge of the person being tracked."*

**That text does not exist.** I pulled the raw guidelines myself (238,359 bytes, HTTP 200, 2026-09-04) and counted: `stalkerware` **0** · `monitor` **0** · `stalk` **0** · `covert` **0** · `persistent notification` **0** · `another person` **0** · `spouse` **0**. The summarizer fabricated a plausible policy in exactly the direction a researcher would hope to find one. Every platform-policy quote in this addendum was verified against raw fetched bytes.

## Corrections to what I told you

**1. I was wrong that Family Link has no child-facing disclosure.** I marked those pages NOT REACHED (CAPTCHA) and said `families.google/familylink/` carried "no transparency statement toward the child." Recovered via Wayback, **`support.google.com/families/answer/9092383`** is written in second person *to the supervised user* and enumerates both sides:

> **What parents can supervise** — "Update some of your account settings · Control Google's data collection with settings like Web & App Activity and Location History · Approve or block apps · Find where you are with your Android devices · Remotely lock devices · Block or allow sites on Chrome · View your YouTube channel activity"
>
> **What parents can't check or do** — "Remotely monitor your screen · Check your past searches · Check your Chrome browsing history · Check your YouTube watch history · Check or reset your account password · Read your emails or messages · Listen to your calls · Delete your account · Erase the data on your device · **Remove your ability to stop supervision**"

That last clause is the strongest exit-adjacent commitment found anywhere in this survey. **But read it precisely against the same page's operative text:** 13–18 — *"You'll need your parent's approval to stop supervision."* 18+ — *"You don't need your parent's approval."* So what is non-removable is the **ability to initiate a request**, not the ability to leave. **It is a petition right that cannot be confiscated, not an exit.** My §B table stands.

**2. The Google Play stalkerware policy is reached, dated, and it is the strongest disclosure obligation in the entire survey.** Effective **1 October 2020** (announced 2020-09-16, `answer/10065487`), now at `answer/9888380` under **Malware** — Google classifies covert monitoring as a potentially harmful application, not a UX defect:

> "Google Play prohibits apps from monitoring another individual… **unless the app is exclusively designed and marketed for parents to monitor their children** or enterprise management…"
> "**These apps cannot be used to track anyone else (a spouse, for example) even with their knowledge and permission, regardless if persistent notification is displayed.**"
> "These apps must use the **`IsMonitoringTool` metadata flag in their manifest file**…"
> "Apps must not present themselves as a spying or secret surveillance solution. · Apps must not hide or cloak tracking behavior… · **Apps must present users with a persistent notification at all times when the app is running and a unique icon that clearly identifies the app.** · Apps must disclose monitoring or tracking functionality in the Google Play store description."

The rule hardened over time from prose into a **machine-checkable manifest declaration**. And note it sits chronologically between the two FTC actions — Retina-X (Oct 2019) → Play policy (Oct 2020) → SpyFone (Sep 2021). Sequence only; causation not documented.

**3. My verdict on "did the platforms choose visible management" was too negative — it was an artifact of blocked pages.** Corrected:

| | Relation disclosed to subject? | **Who** manages disclosed? | Persistent ambient indicator? |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Google Family Link** | **Yes** — dedicated child-facing page + `Settings › Digital Wellbeing & parental controls` carrying the `Stop supervision` control | No — "your parent," generically | **Not documented** |
| **Apple Family Sharing** | **Yes** — and inside the *Personal Safety* guide, i.e. framed for a possibly-coerced reader | **Yes** — *"Settings › [your name] › Family… tap the icon to see your family members and roles"* — the only such affordance found | **Not documented** |
| **Microsoft Family Safety** | Inferable from role structure only; nothing published addressed to the member | No | No — *"**Only Organizers can enable, disable and view activity reports**"* |

## The finding that matters most

**The platforms wrote the persistent-notification rule for everyone but themselves.** Google Play compels an always-on indicator plus unique icon on every third-party monitoring app, enforces it via manifest flag, and forbids monitoring even a *consenting* spouse. **Family Link itself ships no documented ambient indicator** — the relation is discoverable in Settings; it is not announced. Apple writes no such rule at all, and ships the survey's cleanest case of silent supervision (`support.apple.com/en-us/105121`): a restricted app *"is just temporarily hidden from the Home Screen"* — no notice, no explanation, no attribution. The relation stays visible in Settings; every individual *exercise* of it is invisible. Microsoft ships an organizer-only report covering web history and search terms, for members *"of any age"*, with a support page titled **"Family Safety settings enforced on adults."**

Whether the self-exemption is earned — a supervised Google account is *created* supervised, so there is no covert conversion moment to disclose — or simply unexamined, **no published rationale was reachable.**

## Two regulator instruments, and the shape of their remedies

- **FTC v. Retina-X, 2019-10-22.** The pled harm was icon-hiding specifically. The remedy: *"must include an icon with the name of the app on the mobile device, **which is only removable by a parent or legal guardian** who has installed the app on their minor child's phone."* The FTC mandated a marker **the supervised party can see but cannot remove, and the supervisor alone can remove.** Visibility compulsory; removability asymmetric — by design, in the regulator's own remedy.
- **FTC v. Support King / SpyFone, 2021-09-01.** First outright ban, plus a **retrospective** duty: *"notify device owners that the app had been secretly installed."* Levine: *"The stalkerware was hidden from device owners, but was fully exposed to hackers."* Retina-X fixed the future; SpyFone fixed the past.

Both are consent decrees binding named respondents — **not rules of general application.** No general FTC rule requires telling a monitored person. GDPR angle remains NOT REACHED.

## Revised §E bottom line

The supervised party can *discover* the relation in Google and Apple, and is told *who* only by Apple. Nobody is *notified* — not of onset, not of setting changes, not of access. The one enforceable always-on-disclosure rule in existence applies only to third parties, and its sole carve-out is the parent/child case; the FTC's parallel remedy carves the same hole and hands the supervisor the removal key. The three properties I named still hold as the design axis, and this thread sharpens the second: **DULT §4.1.6's "alerts must not be circumventable under any circumstance" and Play's `IsMonitoringTool` are the same commitment expressed twice — and neither platform applies it to its own family system.**

**Still NOT REACHED:** whether Family Link discloses the supervising account's identity; whether any of the three emits an ambient indicator beyond a Settings entry; whether Apple notifies a child when Ask to Buy / Screen Time / location sharing are first enabled; whether Microsoft tells a member reporting was switched on; full text of both FTC Decisions and Orders (all `ftc.gov/system/files/` PDF paths 404); EU/GDPR notice obligations; published platform design rationale.

